# Cameras

`Fp3Camera` takes stills and streams video from the `:rear` and `:front`
cameras.

## Which sensors are fitted?

The camera modules can be swapped, so ask which sensor each slot holds:

```elixir
Fp3Camera.info(:rear)
```

```elixir
Fp3Camera.info(:front)
```

## Take a photo

A full-resolution still (12 MP on the rear) takes about 30 seconds.

```elixir
{:ok, jpeg} = Fp3Camera.snap_bytes(:rear)
Kino.Image.new(jpeg, :jpeg)
```

Faster and smaller, with 2×2 binning:

```elixir
{:ok, jpeg} = Fp3Camera.snap_bytes(:front, binned: true)
Kino.Image.new(jpeg, :jpeg)
```

## Tune the picture

On Android, a dedicated image processor and years of per-sensor tuning
turn the sensor's raw data into a photo. Here that work happens in
software, in the `cam-snap` program: white balance, demosaicing, a tone
curve, sharpening and noise reduction. Every step has a setting, so you
can tune it yourself.

The sliders start at the default values. Change them, then run the next
cell to take a quick (2×2-binned) photo with them:

```elixir
camera = Kino.Input.select("Camera", rear: "Rear", front: "Front")
gamma = Kino.Input.range("Gamma: brightness of the mid-tones", min: 1.0, max: 3.0, step: 0.1, default: 2.2)
contrast = Kino.Input.range("Contrast", min: 0.0, max: 1.0, step: 0.05, default: 0.35)
saturation = Kino.Input.range("Saturation: colour strength", min: 0.0, max: 2.5, step: 0.1, default: 1.5)
brightness = Kino.Input.range("Brightness", min: 0.5, max: 2.5, step: 0.1, default: 1.0)
sharpen = Kino.Input.range("Sharpening (0 = off)", min: 0.0, max: 3.0, step: 0.1, default: 1.0)
denoise = Kino.Input.range("Noise reduction (0 = off)", min: 0, max: 10, step: 1, default: 4)
white_balance =
  Kino.Input.select("White balance",
    default: "Camera default",
    auto: "Automatic (from the picture)",
    fixed: "Fixed gains"
  )

warmth = Kino.Input.range("Warmth: extra red", min: 0.8, max: 1.3, step: 0.01, default: 1.05)

# The rear camera's lens is moved by a motor; the front ones are fixed-focus.
focus_mode = Kino.Input.select("Focus (rear camera)", auto: "Automatic", manual: "Manual")
focus = Kino.Input.range("Manual focus: 0 = far away, 1023 = very close", min: 0, max: 1023, step: 1, default: 300)

Kino.Layout.grid(
  [camera, gamma, contrast, saturation, brightness, sharpen, denoise, white_balance, warmth,
   focus_mode, focus],
  columns: 2
)
```

```elixir
settings = [
  gamma: Kino.Input.read(gamma),
  contrast: Kino.Input.read(contrast),
  saturation: Kino.Input.read(saturation),
  brightness: Kino.Input.read(brightness),
  sharpen: if(Kino.Input.read(sharpen) == 0, do: false, else: Kino.Input.read(sharpen)),
  denoise: round(Kino.Input.read(denoise)),
  warm_bias: Kino.Input.read(warmth)
]

settings =
  case Kino.Input.read(white_balance) do
    :auto -> settings ++ [awb: true]
    :fixed -> settings ++ [awb: false]
    :default -> settings
  end

settings =
  cond do
    Kino.Input.read(camera) == :front -> settings
    Kino.Input.read(focus_mode) == :auto -> settings ++ [focus: :auto]
    true -> settings ++ [focus: round(Kino.Input.read(focus))]
  end

{:ok, jpeg} = Fp3Camera.snap_bytes(Kino.Input.read(camera), [binned: true] ++ settings)
Kino.Image.new(jpeg, :jpeg)
```

`Fp3Camera.snap/3` documents more settings: `:exposure`, `:gain`,
explicit white-balance gains (`wb: {r, g, b}`) and lens shading
correction (`:lsc`). The cameras have no optical zoom.

Happy with the result? Make it the default for this camera's photos and
keep it across reboots:

```elixir
Fp3Camera.Config.put({Kino.Input.read(camera), :snap}, settings)
Fp3Camera.Config.save()
```

To go back to the built-in values:

```elixir
Fp3Camera.Config.reset()
Fp3Camera.Config.save()
```

## Stream video

The stream is raw H.264 over TCP, which a browser can't play. On your
laptop, open it with `ffplay tcp://nerves.local:8888` (or `mpv`).

```elixir
{:ok, stream} = Fp3Camera.start_stream(:rear)
Fp3Camera.streams()
```

Stop it when you're done:

```elixir
Fp3Camera.stop_stream(stream)
```
